When glucose is oxidised the energy is released in small steps and it ends in two forms:

1. **Energy trapped in ATP** (chemical energy). About **one third to two fifths (roughly 34 to 40 per cent)** of the energy of the glucose is **used to make ATP** from ADP and phosphate ($32 \times 30.6\ \mathrm{kJ} \approx 980\ \mathrm{kJ}$ out of $2870\ \mathrm{kJ}$ is about 34 per cent). ATP is the **energy currency** and is used for:
   - **muscle contraction** (mechanical work, movement);
   - **active transport** of ions and molecules across membranes (for example the sodium-potassium pump, absorption of glucose, uptake of mineral ions by roots);
   - **synthesis of large molecules** (proteins, nucleic acids, polysaccharides, lipids), growth, cell division and repair;
   - **nerve impulse conduction** and secretion;
   - in some organisms, **light production** (glow-worm) or electricity (electric fish).
2. **Energy lost as heat** (the rest, about **60 per cent or more**). In **mammals and birds (endotherms)** this heat **maintains a constant, high body temperature**, which keeps enzymes at their optimum. In other organisms it is simply lost to the surroundings.

All the energy that is used finally ends up as heat in the environment, because energy cannot be recycled by the living organism.
